# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Part 1 of the manuscript: introduction, architecture and mathematical model (no numbers computed here beyond Table 1).

# ============================================================================================ 1 Introduction
add(("h1", "1. Introduction"))
add(("p", r"Glucose-lowering agents that also suppress appetite, notably glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) receptor agonists and dual agonists, produce large and sustained weight loss in people with overweight or obesity [@Wilding2021;@Jastreboff2022]. Part of the weight that is lost is lean mass. The frequently quoted estimate that about one quarter of the weight lost is fat-free mass has itself been critically reviewed [@Heymsfield2014], and reviews of incretin-based therapy document lean-mass reductions and ask whether they are adaptive or maladaptive [@Sargeant2019;@Neeland2024;@Linge2024;@Prado2024]. The question this raises is quantitative. Skeletal muscle is the main site of insulin-stimulated glucose disposal [@DeFronzo2009]; low muscle mass is associated with insulin resistance and dysglycemia [@Srikanthan2010] and with mortality in older adults [@Srikanthan2014]; and the consequences of sarcopenia are serious enough to have an agreed clinical definition [@Cruz2019]. An agent that lowers glucose partly by shrinking muscle therefore works against itself on one channel while helping on another [@Kalyani2014;@Wolfe2006]. At what dose does the muscle cost outweigh the glucose benefit, and what determines that dose?"))
add(("p", r"Aging biomarkers cannot answer this question by themselves. Epigenetic clocks read a biological age from DNA methylation [@Horvath2013;@Hannum2013;@Levine2018;@Lu2023], methylation entropy measures the disorder of the same layer [@Chan2025], and loss of epigenetic information has been proposed as a cause of mammalian aging [@Yang2023], a proposal that has been supported by reprogramming experiments [@Lu2020] and has motivated clock-based endpoints in intervention trials [@Fahy2019;@Barzilai2016]. These quantities describe a state. They are estimators fitted to cross-sectional or longitudinal data, and they do not represent the feedback loop that drives the state or predict what a dose that has not been tested would do [@Bell2019]. Dynamical models exist for glucose–insulin regulation [@Bergman1979;@Topp2000] and for body-weight change [@Hall2011], but they do not couple a muscle reserve and a latent aging state to the glucose signal. The geroscience program, which treats aging as the common driver of chronic disease [@Kennedy2014;@LopezOtin2023], would benefit from a small model in which such a coupling can be examined, even if it cannot yet be fitted."))
add(("p", r"This study develops and analyzes such a model. A latent information-loss variable, a muscle reserve and an energy-storage compartment are coupled through circulating glucose, which is held at quasi-steady state: glucose is an output of the system, because it rises as information is lost and as muscle declines, and an input, because it drives information loss through a glycation-like damage term [@Brownlee2001]. A single dose acts on three channels, lowering glucose directly, removing lean mass and reducing the storage compartment. The model is intentionally minimal and is not fitted. It is used to ask four questions. First, under what conditions does an interior optimal dose exist, and can that condition be stated explicitly? Second, how much of its existence is a structural property rather than a property of the chosen parameter values, and which parameters control it? Third, does an adiposity-associated aging cost comparable to the muscle cost change the answer? Fourth, does an accessible, routinely computable index of the glucose-to-muscle balance report the same optimum as the latent variable it is meant to stand for, and what data would be needed to estimate the parameters that matter?"))
add(("p", r"The answers are conditional on the model and are stated here with their qualifiers. {ANSWERS}"))
add(("p", r"The novelty claimed is that of the combination, not of any single element: dynamical models of glucose, of body weight and of aging clocks exist separately, and sensitivity and identifiability analyses are established methods [@Sobol2001;@Saltelli2010;@Marino2008;@Raue2009]. What is new here is an exact decomposition of the dose–response slope into glucose, muscle and storage pathways, a closed-form stationarity condition that predicts the optimum from a ratio of two aging couplings, tests of that result against the sampling prior, the treatment horizon, the functional forms and the removal of each model element, an adiposity penalty that the earlier formulation lacked, and an explicit separation of what is assumed, what is derived and what is only proposed as a surrogate."))
add(("p", r"The status of the results is stated at the outset. This is a theoretical and computational study. No human or animal data were used and no parameter was fitted, so every number is a modeled outcome conditional on the assumptions of Section 3 and Table 1, not a measured effect size or a dosing recommendation. The only data-like quantities are the qualitative targets the untreated course was tuned to reproduce (Section 3.1). Section 5.1 separates the quantities that are assumed, derived and proposed, and Section 5.5 specifies a dose-ranging experiment and the outcomes that would refute the model."))

# ============================================================================================ 2 Architecture
add(("h1", "2. Model architecture and the status of each quantity"))
add(("p", r"The structure of the model is shown in Fig. 1. A constant dose $u$ is applied from age 45 to age 75. It lowers circulating glucose directly, as a glucose-lowering agent does through insulin secretion, glucagon suppression and reduced intake; it removes lean mass in proportion to a saturating function of dose; and it reduces the storage compartment. Information loss is driven by glucose, amplified by a susceptibility that rises with information loss, and by the deficit in muscle reserve; a larger information loss in turn raises glucose, closing the loop."))
add(("fig", "Fig1_structure.png", r"Fig. 1. Structure of the model. A single dose acts on three channels (glucose, muscle, storage). Information loss $I$ is a posited latent state; glucose $G$ is algebraically determined by the states and the dose; SIM and the metabolic age are readouts derived from the states.", 5.9))
add(("h2", "2.1 Posited, state, derived and proxy quantities"))
add(("p", r"A reader needs to know, for each quantity, whether it is assumed, computed from other quantities or only proposed as a stand-in for something that cannot be measured here. Table 2 states this. The information-loss variable $I$ is posited: it is a stand-in for biological aging, not something computed from muscle, fat or glucose, and no measurement is assumed to equal it. Muscle reserve $M$ and the storage compartment $F$ are state variables with their own dynamics. Glucose $G$, by contrast, is fully determined at every instant by $(I, M, F, u)$, because it is treated at quasi-steady state. The index SIM is a further derived readout of $G$, the susceptibility and $M$. The metabolic age is a monotone transformation of a derived readout."))
add(("table", r"Table 2. Status of each quantity of the model. Nothing in the first column other than the intervention dose is measured in this study.",
     ["Quantity", "Role in the model", "Status", "Measurable in principle by"],
     [[r"$I$", "Information loss; drives its own growth", "Posited latent state; not derived from $M$, $F$ or $G$", "Methylation-entropy or clock readout, with an unknown calibration"],
      [r"$M$", "Muscle reserve (young value 1)", "State variable with its own equation", "Lean mass, muscle strength or creatinine-based indices"],
      [r"$F$", "Energy-storage compartment (adiposity)", "State variable with its own equation", "Fat mass, waist circumference"],
      [r"$G$", "Circulating glucose (units of $G_0$)", "Derived: algebraic function of $(I, M, F, u)$", "Fasting glucose or glycated hemoglobin"],
      [r"$\sigma$", "Susceptibility to glucose damage", "Derived: algebraic function of $I$", "Not directly; inflammatory markers are candidates"],
      [r"$\mathrm{SIM}$", "Glucose-susceptibility burden per unit muscle", "Derived readout; a proposed surrogate, not validated", "Candidate panel in Section 5.3"],
      [r"Metabolic age", "SIM or $I$ mapped onto the untreated life course", "Derived by inverting the untreated curve", "Same as the quantity it inverts"],
      [r"$u$", "Constant dose from age 45", "Control input; assumed constant", "Dose of the agent"]],
     [0.9, 2.0, 2.4, 2.4]))
add(("h2", "2.2 Three outcomes that are not the same thing"))
add(("p", r"Three outcomes are used and must be kept apart. The first is the information loss at the horizon, $I(75)$, the quantity the model is built to describe. The second is the index SIM at the horizon, which is computable from $G$, $\sigma$ and $M$. The third is the metabolic age, which maps either of the two onto the age at which the untreated model reaches the same value. The metabolic age from SIM is the quantity most likely to be reported in practice, and the metabolic age from $I$ is the one that corresponds to the model's own definition of aging. They need not have the same minimum, nor the same magnitude of benefit, and Section 4.3 shows that they do not."))
add(("h2", "2.3 Slowing information loss is not reversing it"))
add(("p", r"The factor $(1 - I)$ in the equation for $I$ and the non-negativity of every rate make $I$ non-decreasing and bounded by 1. An intervention in this model can therefore only slow the accumulation of information loss. It cannot return $I$ to a younger value. This is a deliberate simplification suited to a soma that has lost the capacity to repair its regulatory information, and it limits the interpretation of every result: a lower $I(75)$ means less accumulated loss, a smaller metabolic age means a later position on the untreated curve, and neither means reversal of aging [@Lu2020]."))

# ============================================================================================ 3 Model
add(("h1", "3. Mathematical model and analysis"))
add(("p", r"The state is $(I, M, F)$ with $I \in [0,1]$, $M \in (0,1]$ and $F > 0$. Circulating glucose $G$ and the susceptibility $\sigma$ are algebraic functions of the state and the dose $u$. Time is age in years. Symbols are defined in Table 1."))
add(("eq", r"G = G_0\,\frac{1 + a_I\, I + a_F\, F}{1 + a_M\, M + a_u\, u}", 1))
add(("eq", r"\sigma(I) = 1 + c_{\sigma}\, I", 2))
add(("eq", r"\frac{dI}{dt} = \left[\, r_0 + r_g\, G\,\sigma(I) + r_m\,(1 - M)^{p_m} + r_f\,\Phi(F) \right](1 - I)", 3))
add(("eq", r"\frac{dM}{dt} = k_M\left[(1 - \lambda I)\left(1 - \varphi\, H(u)\right) - M\right], \qquad H(u) = \frac{u^{n_H}}{k_u^{\,n_H} + u^{n_H}}", 4))
add(("eq", r"\frac{dF}{dt} = g_F\left(F_{\mathrm{set}}(I) - F\right) - c_F\, u\, F, \qquad F_{\mathrm{set}}(I) = 0.4 + 0.4\, I", 5))
add(("eq", r"\Phi(F) = \left[\max\left(F - F_{\mathrm{ref}},\, 0\right)\right]^{p_f}", 6))
add(("p", r"Equation (1) expresses glucose as production over a disposal capacity, the standard quasi-steady-state form for a pool that equilibrates in minutes to hours while $I$, $M$ and $F$ evolve over years to decades, a separation of roughly five to seven orders of magnitude. Production rises with information loss and with the storage compartment. The disposal capacity falls with muscle reserve, which is the primary sink for glucose [@DeFronzo2009;@Srikanthan2010], and with the dose of the agent. Equation (2) makes susceptibility to glucose-related damage rise linearly with information loss, a first-order expansion of the loss of reserve. Equation (3) has four terms. The first is a baseline turnover. The second is damage proportional to glucose and susceptibility, a glycation-like mechanism [@Brownlee2001]. The third is a cost of the muscle deficit, a power of the fractional loss of reserve, motivated by the association of low muscle mass with mortality and frailty [@Srikanthan2014;@Cruz2019]. The fourth is a cost of storage above its reference value, absent in the base model ($r_f = 0$) and introduced in Section 4.7 because adipose tissue is itself inflammatory and senescence-associated [@Hotamisligil2006;@Tchkonia2010;@Batsis2018]. The factor $(1 - I)$ makes $I = 1$ absorbing."))
add(("p", r"Equation (4) relaxes muscle reserve to a set point that falls with information loss, reduced by the agent through a sigmoidal, saturating dose dependence of lean-mass loss, a Hill function with exponent $n_H$, $H(u)$. Equation (5) relaxes the storage compartment to a set point that rises with information loss, and the agent removes storage at rate $c_F u$. These forms are the simplest that encode known qualitative behavior; they are assumptions, and Section 4.7 replaces each of them."))
add(("table", r"Table 1. Parameters of the model. All values are illustrative: they were chosen so that the untreated life course reproduces the qualitative targets of Section 3.1, and none was fitted. The sampling range is that of the base ensemble (Table 3). Units: $G$ and the dose are dimensionless; rates are per year.",
     ["Symbol", "Meaning", "Nominal", "Role"],
     [[r"$r_0$", "Baseline turnover rate of information loss", "0.0020", "Intercept of the rate"],
      [r"$r_g$", "Glucose-driven information-loss coefficient", "0.0130", "Glucose channel"],
      [r"$r_m$", "Muscle-deficit information-loss coefficient", "0.0400", "Muscle channel"],
      [r"$c_\sigma$", "Susceptibility feedback on $I$", "0.90", "Amplifies the glucose channel"],
      [r"$a_I, a_F$", "Glucose production from $I$ and from $F$", "0.80, 0.45", "Glucose output"],
      [r"$a_M, a_u$", "Glucose disposal by muscle and by the agent", "1.00, 0.55", "Muscle sink; direct benefit"],
      [r"$\lambda$", "Age-related decline of the muscle set point", "0.28", "Muscle trajectory"],
      [r"$k_M$", "Muscle relaxation rate", "0.45 per year", "Muscle timescale"],
      [r"$\varphi, k_u, n_H$", "Maximum fractional lean-mass loss; half-maximal dose; Hill exponent", "0.50, 1.00, 2.0", "Drug-induced muscle loss"],
      [r"$p_m$", "Exponent of the muscle-deficit cost", "2.0", "Shape of the muscle channel"],
      [r"$g_F, c_F$", "Storage relaxation rate; drug-induced storage loss rate", "0.055 per year, 0.16", "Storage dynamics"],
      [r"$r_f, p_f, F_{\mathrm{ref}}$", "Storage penalty coefficient, exponent and reference", "0 (base), 1, 0.4", "Adiposity penalty (Section 4.7)"],
      [r"$G_0$", "Glucose normalization", "1", "Units of $G$"]],
     [1.3, 3.6, 1.4, 1.9]))
add(("h2", "3.1 Calibration of the untreated course"))
add(("p", r"The untreated life course, from age 25 to 75 with initial state $(I, M, F) = (0.10, 1, 0.40)$, was tuned to reproduce three qualitative targets of the earlier formulation of the model: an information loss that rises below saturation, a loss of about one sixth of muscle reserve, and a rise of circulating glucose by about one half. These are the only data-like constraints on the parameters. They are literature-consistent in direction and rough magnitude, not fitted values, and Section 4.11 shows how little the observable quantities constrain the parameters that matter. The dose is constant from age 45 to 75 unless stated, and the dose scale is set by $k_u$, so that $u = 1$ is the dose at which lean-mass loss is half its maximum."))
add(("h2", "3.2 Derived quantities and the objective"))
add(("p", r"The index SIM and the two metabolic ages are defined by Eqs. (7) and (8). The objective of the analysis is the information loss at the horizon as a function of the constant dose, Eq. (9)."))
add(("eq", r"\mathrm{SIM} = \frac{G\,\sigma(I)}{M}", 7))
add(("eq", r"a_{\mathrm{SIM}}(u) = \mathrm{SIM}_0^{-1}\left(\mathrm{SIM}(75;\,u)\right), \qquad a_{I}(u) = I_0^{-1}\left(I(75;\,u)\right)", 8))
add(("eq", r"J(u) = I(75;\,u)", 9))
add(("p", r"In Eq. (8), the subscript 0 denotes the untreated course, and a value outside its range is assigned the nearest end of the range, so that metabolic ages lie between 25 and 75. SIM is monotone in age in the untreated model, so its inverse exists. SIM is not a measurement: it is the ratio, in the model, of the glucose burden multiplied by susceptibility to the protective reserve, and Section 5.3 distinguishes its theoretical definition from a candidate observable version."))
add(("h2", "3.3 Conditions for an interior optimum"))
add(("p", r"An interior optimum exists when $J(u)$ has a minimum strictly between $u = 0$ and the largest dose considered, $u_{\max} = 2$. To analyze it, the single dose is split into three channel doses, $u_G$ in the denominator of Eq. (1), $u_M$ in $H(u)$ of Eq. (4) and $u_F$ in the storage term of Eq. (5), which are set equal at the end. By the chain rule the slope of the objective along the common dose is exactly the sum of the three channel derivatives:"))
add(("eq", r"\frac{dJ}{du} = \frac{\partial J}{\partial u_G} + \frac{\partial J}{\partial u_M} + \frac{\partial J}{\partial u_F}, \qquad u_G = u_M = u_F = u", 10))
add(("p", r"In the nominal case and with the adiposity penalty of Section 4.7 (Fig. 3B) the glucose and storage derivatives are negative at every dose (the agent lowers glucose and storage, both of which reduce the damage rate), whereas the muscle derivative is positive. At an interior minimum $u^*$ the sum vanishes, which states a balance between the glucose benefit, the storage benefit and the muscle cost, and the second derivative is non-negative. Two properties follow directly from the structure of the equations. First, for a Hill exponent $n_H > 1$ the function $H(u)$ grows as $u^{n_H}$ near zero, so $\partial J/\partial u_M \to 0$ as $u \to 0$, whereas $\partial J/\partial u_G$ and $\partial J/\partial u_F$ remain strictly negative; hence $J'(0) < 0$ and a small dose always helps. Second, because $J$ is continuous on a closed interval and $J'(0) < 0$, the global minimum is interior if and only if $J(u_{\max}) > \min_u J(u)$, which requires the total slope to be positive at some dose below $u_{\max}$. The decomposition of Eq. (10) is computed numerically by central differences on the three channel doses and agrees with the derivative of $J$ along the common dose to better than $10^{-6}$ (Appendix B)."))
add(("p", r"A closed-form condition is obtained in the quasi-static limit, in which $I$ is frozen and $M$ and $F$ have relaxed to their set points. The information-loss rate at fixed $I$ is then a function $R(u)$ of dose alone, with $M^*(u) = (1 - \lambda I)(1 - \varphi H(u))$, $F^*(u) = g_F F_{\mathrm{set}}/(g_F + c_F u)$ and $G^*(u)$ from Eq. (1). Setting $R'(u) = 0$ in the base model ($r_f = 0$) gives a stationarity condition in terms of the ratio of the two aging couplings:"))
add(("eq", r"\frac{r_m}{r_g} = \rho_c(u;\,I) \equiv \frac{\sigma(I)\left(-\partial G^*/\partial u\right)}{p_m\,\left(1 - M^*\right)^{p_m - 1}\left(-\partial M^*/\partial u\right)}", 11))
add(("p", r"The left side is a property of the organism's aging couplings; the right side is a function of dose and of the state that the agent determines. At small dose $M^*$ changes as $u^{n_H}$, so $\rho_c \to \infty$ and $R'(u) < 0$: a small dose always helps. The sign of $R'(u)$ equals the sign of $r_m/r_g - \rho_c(u; I)$, so a local interior minimum of the rate exists if and only if $r_m/r_g$ exceeds the minimum of $\rho_c$ over the dose range, $\rho_{\min}(I)$, and the first dose at which the two sides are equal is that minimum. For the nominal parameters $\rho_c$ falls from large values at small dose to $\rho_{\min}$ near $u = 1$ and rises slowly beyond it, {RHOMIN}. The condition is exact only in the quasi-static limit; because $I$ and the other parameters vary across a population, it defines a transition in the ratio rather than a sharp threshold, and Section 4.4 tests it against the full model."))
add(("h2", "3.4 Numerical methods"))
add(("p", r"Equations (1)–(6) are integrated with a fixed-step fourth-order Runge–Kutta scheme at a step of 0.125 years, vectorized over the parameter sets. The scheme was checked against an adaptive LSODA integrator [@Petzold1983] from SciPy [@Virtanen2020] with a relative tolerance of $10^{-10}$ and against step-size halving (Appendix B). The dose axis from 0 to 2 is sampled at 41 points, and a parabolic refinement through the grid minimum gives the optimal dose. A parameterization is classified as having an interior optimum when the grid minimum lies strictly inside the dose range and both the benefit, $I(75;0) - \min_u J$, and the backfire, $I(75;u_{\max}) - \min_u J$, exceed $10^{-3}$, a threshold varied in Section 4.5. The calculations use NumPy [@Harris2020] and SciPy, and the figures Matplotlib [@Hunter2007]."))
add(("h2", "3.5 Ensembles, sensitivity and identifiability"))
add(("p", r"The base ensemble reproduces the sampling design of the earlier formulation: each scale parameter of Table 1 is drawn log-uniformly over a factor of four centered on its nominal value, and the exponents $n_H$ and $p_m$ are drawn uniformly on $[1, 3]$ (Table 3). Four further priors and a quasi-random sequence are used to test dependence on this choice. All ensembles are seeded with NumPy SeedSequence from a master seed, so every number can be regenerated, and fractions are reported with Wilson 95% intervals. The half-width of the 95% interval of a fraction near one half is about 0.5 percentage points at 40,000 draws."))
add(("table", r"Table 3. Specification of the ensembles. Every quantity is an assumed sampling choice and not a measurement; Section 4.5 shows the dependence of the main result on each choice.",
     ["Quantity", "Distribution", "Range", "Used for"],
     [["Scale parameters of Table 1 (14)", "Log-uniform around nominal", "0.5×–2× (base); 0.25×–4× (wide)", "Base ensemble; prior test"],
      [r"$n_H$, $p_m$", "Uniform", "[1, 3] (base); [1, 4] (wide)", "Base ensemble; prior test"],
      ["Scale parameters", "Uniform around nominal", "0.5×–1.5×", "Prior test"],
      ["Scale parameters", "Log-normal", r"$\sigma_{\ln} = 0.25$", "Prior test"],
      ["Scale parameters", "Scrambled Sobol sequence", "As base", "Low-discrepancy design"],
      [r"$\varphi$, $\lambda$", "Capped", "At most 0.98 and 0.9, so that the muscle set point stays positive", "All ensembles"],
      ["Dose", "Grid", "0–2 in steps of 0.05", "All ensembles"],
      ["Treatment duration", "Fixed", "5, 10, 20, 30 years from age 45", "Horizon test"],
      [r"Fat-to-muscle penalty ratio $\psi$", "Fixed", "0, 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 4", "Adiposity test"],
      ["Draws", "–", "40,000 (base); 20,000 (priors, horizons, adiposity, ablation); 16,384 (Sobol-sequence prior); 10,000 (functional forms); 6,000 (earlier design); 73,728 (Sobol indices)", "–"]],
     [2.4, 1.9, 2.6, 1.9]))
add(("p", r"Global sensitivity is quantified with first-order and total Sobol indices [@Sobol2001], estimated with the Saltelli design and the Jansen total-effect estimator [@Saltelli2010;@Jansen1999] over the 16 parameters of the base ensemble with $N = 4096$ base samples and 95% intervals from 300 bootstrap resamples; the outputs are the backfire depth, the optimal dose, the benefit, and the indicator of an interior optimum. The estimator is tested on a function with known indices (Appendix B). Practical identifiability is analyzed with the Fisher information matrix of the log-parameters at the nominal point for hypothetical cohort-mean observations, with a Gaussian prior of standard deviation 0.40 on each log-parameter, the standard deviation of a log-uniform distribution over a factor of four, and the information gain is $1 - \mathrm{SD}_{post}/\mathrm{SD}_{prior}$ [@Raue2009]. The observation sets and noise levels are assumptions of the analysis, listed in Section 4.11."))
